-- title:
--   Comaximal factors of an invertible ideal sheaf are invertible
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and let $I,J$ be quasi-coherent ideal sheaf data on $X$ (Mathlib's `Scheme.IdealSheafData`, i.e. a compatible assignment of an ideal $I.\mathrm{ideal}(U) \subseteq \Gamma(X,U)$ to each affine open $U$). Assume, first, that the product $I * J$ is invertible in the sense of the predicate `IsInvertible`: for every point $x$ of $X$ there are an affine open $U$ of $X$ and a section $f \in \Gamma(X,U)$ with $x$ in the basic open $X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\, f$, together with an element $g$ of $\Gamma(X, X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f)$ that is a non-zero-divisor and satisfies $(I * J).\mathrm{ideal}(X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\, f) = (g)$; and, second, that $I \sqcup J = \top$ in the lattice of ideal sheaf data, i.e. $I$ and $J$ are comaximal. The conclusion is that $I$ and $J$ are each invertible in exactly the same sense: each point of $X$ has a basic affine open neighbourhood on which the ideal in question is generated by a single non-zero-divisor.
--
--   This is the scheme-theoretic form of the elementary fact that a factorisation of an effective Cartier divisor into comaximal ideal sheaves has invertible factors; the invertibility here is the local principality-by-a-non-zero-divisor condition defining effective Cartier divisors. It is used in the construction of resolutions attached to crossing quotients, where an invertible ideal sheaf must be split along a comaximal decomposition, via [`MvPolynomial.CrossingQuotient.Resolution.exists_open_pullback_twist_iso_tensorUnit_of_degree_eq_zero`](thm.html#MvPolynomial.CrossingQuotient.Resolution.exists_open_pullback_twist_iso_tensorUnit_of_degree_eq_zero).
